# Public API base (server-side calls go to FastAPI)
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8000/api/v1
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000
# Production (Vercel): https://www.sortedchoice.com — the `www` is deliberate,
# it is the Production domain; the apex and *.vercel.app both redirect to it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase (client-side auth)
#
# Only these two may ever be NEXT_PUBLIC_. The anon key is designed to be
# public — Row Level Security is what protects the data.
#
# NEVER put SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY here. It bypasses RLS entirely and
# belongs in the FastAPI environment only.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://localhost:54321
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Fixtures. OPT-IN, and deliberately so.
#
# Only the exact strings "1" and "true" turn mock data on. Absent, empty,
# "0", "false", or a typo all mean REAL API. The polarity used to be the other
# way round, and production shipped an entire fabricated catalogue for weeks
# because this variable was simply never set on Vercel — the fixtures share
# slugs with the real rows, so every page resolved and looked correct.
#
# Two things enforce this beyond the default:
#   - A production build REFUSES to start with mocks on (src/lib/env.ts).
#   - `npm run build` fails if fixture data reaches the bundle with mocks off
#     (scripts/assert-no-mocks.mjs), so the guarantee is tested, not asserted.
#
# With mocks on, /styleguide works and NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL is unused.
# With mocks off, NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL is REQUIRED — an unset value fails the
# build rather than quietly pointing at localhost from a deployed instance.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_USE_MOCKS=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google Analytics 4. OPTIONAL, and normally left unset.
#
# The measurement ID lives in `src/lib/analytics.ts` as a constant, not here.
# It is public by construction — it ships in the HTML of every page, which is
# how the tag works — so there is nothing to protect by keeping it out of the
# repo, and a real cost to doing so: this project already shipped a public
# feature that was env-gated, unset on Vercel, and silently off in production
# for weeks (see the NEXT_PUBLIC_USE_MOCKS note above).
#
# Set this only to deviate from that default:
#   unset   → the property in lib/analytics.ts. The normal case.
#   G-XXXX  → a different property, e.g. a throwaway one on preview deploys so
#             test traffic stays out of the real numbers.
#   (empty) → GA off entirely. Nothing is injected, no request reaches Google,
#             no cookie can be set, and the GA hosts drop out of the CSP.
#
# Consent: OPT-OUT. The tag boots with Consent Mode v2 and `analytics_storage:
# granted` unless the reader has switched it off at /account/settings, and the
# basis is legitimate interest rather than consent — /privacy and /cookies say
# so in public, so they are part of this and not optional documentation. It was
# opt-in first and reported nothing at all: GA4 does not surface consent-denied
# traffic in Realtime or the standard reports. Advertising signals are denied at
# boot and never granted from anywhere. See src/lib/analytics.ts for the
# conditions this basis depends on before changing any of it.
#
# The site's OWN analytics — /api/track, the admin Analytics screen — are a
# separate system entirely and are unaffected by any value here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_ID=
